= Research: Toward a predictive science of deep learning

My goal is to uncover the principles governing deep learning and use them to explain mechanisms, make quantitative predictions, and inform algorithm design. I pursue this goal through three complementary perspectives:

- *Physics.* Finding the right level of description: deciding which details matter at a given scale and building toy models to isolate the underlying mechanisms.

- *Mathematics.* Establishing rigorous results under explicit conditions and clarifying the scope of a theory.

- *Engineering.* Stress-testing theory in real-world systems and refining it through discrepancies between predictions and observations.

Inspired by physics, I view *scale as an organizing principle for deep learning*. I do not expect a single theory to capture learning across all scales; different scales require different [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effective_theory effective theories].

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="research-figure">
<img src="./doc/physics-dl.svg" alt="Different scales, different effective theories in physics and deep learning" style="display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 1000px; height: auto; margin: 0 auto;" />
</div>
~~~

This perspective suggests two complementary routes toward understanding deep learning:

- *Bottom-up.* Starting from simple models and microscopic mechanisms and working toward more complex learning systems.
- *Top-down.* Starting from macroscopic laws and using them to guide the study of underlying mechanisms and intermediate-scale dynamics.

Much of my earlier work followed the bottom-up route. I have come to see a limitation of developing theory from microscopic models alone: without constraints from macroscopic observations, it is difficult to determine which modeling choices and mechanisms are relevant to real-world training. I am now exploring the top-down route, using observed regularities to guide toy model construction and analysis, with the hope that the two approaches will eventually inform one another.

== Current focus: Starting from LLM pretraining

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="research-focus-grid">
<div class="research-focus-text">
~~~

LLM pretraining minimizes the next-token prediction loss over vast text corpora:

\(
  \min_{\theta} \mathcal{R}(\theta),\qquad
  \mathcal{R}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{D}}
  \left[-\sum_{k=2}^{L}\log P_{\theta}(x_k\mid x_1,\dots,x_{k-1})\right],
\)

where $P_\theta$ is a conditional token distribution parameterized by a large Transformer, and $\mathcal{D}$ is a distribution over sequences from internet-scale corpora.
Despite its apparent complexity, pretraining exhibits simple macroscopic regularities, such as [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_scaling_law neural scaling laws]. My research aims to understand how these regularities emerge and how they can guide more efficient training from three perspectives:



- *Macroscopic laws.* Identify collective variables that capture training dynamics and quantitative laws that govern their evolution.

- *Microscopic mechanisms.* Understand how high-dimensional learning dynamics give rise to these laws and how optimization algorithms shape them. When does momentum help, and why can Adam outperform SGD?

- *Practical training.* Use these laws and mechanisms to design more efficient training methods and guide hyperparameter scaling.

For an overview, see my [./doc/LLM-pretraining-theory.pdf talk slides]. Selected work includes:

- *Functional scaling law (FSL).* [https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19189 NeurIPS 2025], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14208 ICLR 2026],  [https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06797 COLT 2026].

- *Effective learning rate (ELR) collapse.*  [https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24814 arXiv 2026].

- *Optimization mechanisms and algorithm design.*  [https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02728 arXiv 2026],  [https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19002 ICML 2025].

